Ladybird links its bundled FFmpeg at startup and loads a system
libavcodec beside it only when that library’s libavutil can’t be
confused with the bundled one: Under glibc, the bundled libraries’
own version nodes keep the two apart, and elsewhere, the libavutil
majors have to match. Gecko avoids the problem a different way: Its
FFVPXRuntimeLinker loads the bundled libmozavcodec and libmozavutil,
and its FFmpegRuntimeLinker loads the system libavcodec, both with
PR_LD_LOCAL (RTLD_LOCAL), so neither library’s symbols ever reach the
global namespace, and each libavcodec binds to its own libavutil —
symbol versions or not. Blink links a single FFmpeg at build time and
never loads another at runtime, and WebKit decodes through GStreamer’s
gst-libav, with no bundled FFmpeg beside it. (From reading the source,
not from running those engines on musl.)
